00:01:27,096 --> 00:01:33,096
Shrek is a 2001 American animated fantasy comedy film directed by Andrew Adamson and Vicky
Jenson, and written by Ted Elliott, Terry Rossio, Joe Stillman, and Roger S.

10
00:01:36,774 --> 00:01:42,774
Schulman, loosely based on the 1990 children's picture book Shrek!

11
00:01:46,451 --> 00:01:52,451
by William Steig.

12
00:01:56,129 --> 00:02:02,129
It is the first installment in the Shrek film series, and stars Mike
Myers, Eddie Murphy, Cameron Diaz and John Lithgow.

13
00:02:05,806 --> 00:02:11,806
In the film, an embittered ogre named Shrek (voiced by Myers) finds his home in the swamp overrun
by fairy tale creatures banished by the obsessive ruler Lord Farquaad (Lithgow).

14
00:02:15,483 --> 00:02:21,483
With the help of Donkey (Murphy), Shrek makes a pact with Farquaad to rescue
Princess Fiona (Diaz) in exchange for regaining control of his swamp.

15
00:02:25,161 --> 00:02:31,161
After purchasing the rights to Steig's book in 1991, Steven Spielberg sought to
produce a traditionally-animated film adaptation, but John H.

16
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:40,838
Williams convinced him to bring the project to the
newly founded DreamWorks Pictures in 1994.

17
00:02:44,516 --> 00:02:50,516
Jeffrey Katzenberg, along with Williams and Aron Warner, began development on Shrek in 1995,
immediately following the studio's purchase of the rights from Spielberg.

18
00:02:54,193 --> 00:03:00,193
Chris Farley was cast as the voice for the title character, recording most of the required dialogue, but died in 1997 before his
work on the film was finished; Myers was hired to replace him, and gave Shrek his characteristic Scottish accent.

19
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:09,870
The film was initially intended to be created using motion capture, but after poor test results,
the studio hired Pacific Data Images to complete the final computer animation.

20
00:03:13,548 --> 00:03:19,548
Shrek parodies other fairy tale adaptations, primarily animated Disney films.

21
00:03:23,225 --> 00:03:29,225
Shrek premiered at the Mann Village Theatre in Westwood on April 22, 2001, and was later shown at the 2001 Cannes Film Festival, where it competed for
the Palme d'Or, making it the first animated film since Picha's The Missing Link (1980) to be chosen as main competition at the festival.

22
00:03:32,903 --> 00:03:38,903
The film was theatrically released by DreamWorks Pictures in the United States on May 18, 2001, and
grossed over $492 million worldwide, becoming the fourth highest-grossing film of 2001.

23
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:48,580
It was widely praised by critics for its animation, voice performances, soundtrack,
writing and humor, which they noted catered to both adults and children.

24
00:03:52,258 --> 00:03:58,258
Shrek was named one of the top 10 films of 2001 by the American Film Institute, the first animated film to be listed, and won numerous
accolades including the BAFTA Award for Best Adapted Screenplay and the inaugural Academy Award for Best Animated Feature.

25
00:04:01,935 --> 00:04:07,935
The film's major success helped establish DreamWorks Animation as a
competitor to Pixar in feature film computer animation.

26
00:04:11,612 --> 00:04:17,612
Three sequels have been released—Shrek 2 (2004), Shrek the Third (2007), and Shrek Forever After (2010)—along with two spin-off
films—Puss in Boots (2011) and Puss in Boots: The Last Wish (2022)—with other productions, notably Shrek 5, in development.

27
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:27,290
It is also regarded as one of the most influential animated films of the
2000s and one of the greatest animated films ever made.

28
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:36,967
The United States Library of Congress selected Shrek for preservation in the National Film
Registry in 2020, becoming the first animated film of the 21st century to be preserved.

29
00:04:40,645 --> 00:04:46,645
The film explores themes of karaoke, swamp, dj, woman director, ogre.

30
00:04:50,322 --> 00:04:56,322
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
